  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Definition
    
    Shale☽ Veil is a Nightshelf practice of deliberate descent into the Berg's oldest ice strata — pressures deep enough to distort light — for the purpose of reading frozen memory directly. It belongs to the third and least understood school of Berg magic: pressure-depth communion. It is neither aurora-light divination nor fire-core transformation. The ice-memory literature suggests it predates both.
    
    ## Discovery
    
    Glow-Trader Vemlaus-vel~, a Nightshelf merchant active during the Frozen Peace, discovered the practice by accident near The Dawn Terrace, the aurora-light repository where the boundary between surface and depth geology is unusually thin. She emerged three days later with white streaking her feathers and an ability to speak in prophecies that found no willing audience. The account is consistent across sources, though 'three days' is suspiciously precise for someone who was alone underground.
    
    Vemlaus-vel~ subsequently documented the practice in The Scared~ Shroud, a fire-core tome bound in volcanic glass and ancestor bone. The text is encoded. Even Nightshelf shadow-workers who can read it approach the material with considerable caution.
    
    ## Mechanism and Cost
    
    Practitioners expose themselves to the accumulated weight of deep ice until perception fractures. The ice yields its memories — orca blood, vanished colonies, the grinding of the Berg's foundation against something below that no source adequately names. These visions do not dissipate cleanly; practitioners describe them as persisting behind the eyes like scar tissue.
    
    The exchange is not voluntary. The ice takes a memory from the user — reportedly one the user needed. Shale☽ Veil does not grant power as miners or channelers understand it. It grants burden.
    
    ## Cultural Trace
    
    In the lower Nightshelf tunnels, children have encoded the practice into dare games — pressing flippers to deep-ice walls and claiming to see *old dead birds who used to be.* Their rhymes warn of *hollow eyes for them that dared* and repeat the refrain *don't look, the ice looks back.* Parents who overhear pull their chicks away. The children do not understand why. The parents, one suspects, understand too well.
